<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Como</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lario</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Como is a glacial lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, shaped like an inverted Y and fed and drained by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adda">Adda</a> river. One of the deepest lakes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, it lies on routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> to the Alpine passes, which gave the city of Como at its southern tip strategic importance. Its shores are known for villas built from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> onwards.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-como.jpg" alt="Lake Como" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luca Casartelli · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sentiero_del_Viandante_DSC_6340_(14020554463).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
